<page "sides usage" notebook>
	<header level="1">
		Sides
		<header>Sides edit four lengths at once, top, left, right and bottom, as a margin or a padding:  a number box per side.</>
	</>
	<section>
		<toolbar class="ui-docs-section-tabs" appearance="segmented" aria-label="Sections">
			<item link="#using-it">Using it</>
			<item link="#keyboard">Keyboard</>
			<item link="#accessibility">Accessibility</>
		</>
	</>

	<!-- USAGE:  how to use it (attributes in practice, events, framework snippets), keyboard, accessibility. -->
	<section label="Using it">
		<markdown>
			- `value` takes CSS's text, one to four numbers in CSS's order (top right bottom left), or an object of
			  side names, `{ top, left, right, bottom }`:  as the property, or as JSON text in the attribute.
			- It gives back the form it was given:  text in, text out;  an object in, an object out.
			- `sides="top bottom"` (or `left right`) shows only those, in one row.
			- In a form it submits CSS's text of all four, an empty side `0`.
		</>
		<markdown>
			```js
			document.querySelector("ui-sides").addEventListener("ui-changed", (event) => console.log(event.detail.value))
			```
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Keyboard">
		<markdown>Tab moves from box to box;  each is a native number box, so the arrow keys step its length.</>
	</>

	<section label="Accessibility">
		<markdown>
			- A group (`role="group"`), named by its `aria-label` or `<label for>`.
			- Each box is named by its side (Top, Left ...);  the letters inside are hidden from screen readers.
		</>
	</>
</page>
